# Small Business Network Security & Segmentation Lab

## Overview

This lab was designed as a small business network environment focused on network segmentation, access control, wireless separation, server infrastructure, and security monitoring.

The goal was to build a realistic network where different departments and device types are separated using VLANs, while still allowing the traffic each group actually needs.

The environment includes employee systems, management devices, servers, security cameras, VoIP phones, guest Wi-Fi, a DMZ, honeypots, a firewall, routing, and access control rules.

## Environment

The lab was built using NetworkSim in a browser-based network simulation environment.

The topology includes:

- Layer 3 switching
- Layer 2 network segmentation
- Router and firewall
- Multiple VLANs
- Employee and management wireless networks
- Guest Wi-Fi
- DNS, FTP, HTTP/HTTPS, and NAS services
- Network printer
- Security cameras and NVR
- VoIP phones
- DMZ
- Honeypot systems
- ACL-based traffic filtering

## Network Design

The network was separated into the following VLANs:

| VLAN | Purpose | Network |
|------|---------|---------|
| 10 | Servers | 10.0.1.0/24 |
| 20 | Security | 10.0.2.0/24 |
| 30 | Management | 10.0.3.0/24 |
| 40 | Staff | 10.0.4.0/24 |
| 50 | Voice | 10.0.5.0/24 |
| 60 | Guest | 10.0.6.0/24 |
| 70 | DMZ | 10.0.7.0/24 |

## Topology

The environment was designed around a central Layer 3 switching and routing structure with firewall protection at the network edge.

The internal network contains separate VLANs for users, infrastructure, security devices, voice systems, and wireless access.

The DMZ contains honeypot systems that are intentionally isolated from the internal network.

Guest Wi-Fi is also separated from the corporate network and is only allowed to access the internet.

## Servers and Services

The server VLAN contains multiple systems providing different services:

- FTP server
- DNS server
- HTTP/HTTPS server
- NAS server
- Network printer

The security VLAN contains the IP security cameras and an NVR used for video recording and storage.

## Wireless Network

The wireless environment includes separate SSIDs for different user groups.

The staff wireless access point provides:

- Management SSID mapped to VLAN 30
- Staff SSID mapped to VLAN 40

The guest wireless access point is mapped to VLAN 60 and is separated from the internal network.

Guest devices are permitted to access the internet but are blocked from accessing internal systems.

## DMZ and Honeypot

The DMZ is placed in VLAN 70 and contains:

- Two honeypot endpoints
- One honeypot server

The honeypot environment is intentionally separated from internal systems.

ACL rules prevent the DMZ from initiating connections into the staff, management, server, voice, and security VLANs.

This helps limit lateral movement if a honeypot system is compromised.

## Access Control

The network uses a deny-by-default ACL policy.

Traffic is only permitted where it is required.

Examples include:

- Management can access internal infrastructure and servers
- Staff can access the internet and approved internal services
- Guest devices can access the internet only
- Guest devices cannot access internal VLANs
- DMZ systems cannot access internal VLANs
- Security devices are isolated from staff systems
- Staff systems cannot access the management network

## Security Goals

The main security goals of the lab were:

- Reduce unnecessary communication between systems
- Separate users and device types using VLANs
- Restrict guest access
- Protect management systems
- Isolate security cameras
- Separate VoIP traffic
- Protect internal systems from the DMZ
- Demonstrate least-privilege network access

## What I Learned

This lab helped me better understand the difference between physical network connectivity and logical network segmentation.

Even though multiple systems may connect through the same switching infrastructure, VLANs can separate them into different broadcast domains.

I also learned that VLANs alone do not provide complete security. Once routing between VLANs is enabled, ACLs or firewall rules are needed to control which networks are allowed to communicate.

Another important part of the lab was understanding how guest networks and DMZ systems should be isolated from trusted internal systems.

